From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sun, 21 Jun 2026 21:05:44 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 03339/11362] [outer-loop] arc: piece-1 measurement OVERTURNS
 premise -- e3 induced models predict near-identity

Wired the cell-recall verify gate into the hidden-state branch too (_score_accuracy in
arc_world_model_trust_energy.py; the gap-1 failing games cn04/ar25/sc25/sk48/wa30 are ALL
hidden-state and gate there, not the non-hidden branch my first commit covered). Parity 10/10 +
trust-energy tests green.

But scoring the SAVED induced world-models directly (arc_e3_induced_model_quality.py, offline, no
LLM) overturns the piece-1 premise: on the failing games cell_recall is ~0-0.05 -- LOWER than the
exact-match accuracy (0.13-0.35), which was INFLATED by no-op transitions (identity is 'correct' when
the grid does not change). The LLM-induced models predict NEAR-IDENTITY: cn04 0.133->0.015, sc25
0.350->0.055, cd82 0.192->0.000, ka59 0.167->0.463; only ar25 is good (0.667->0.857) yet still does
not solve (pieces 2-3). So the cell-recall gate does NOT un-gate e3 -- it correctly REJECTS useless
models. The e3 bottleneck is INDUCTION QUALITY (model-independent gemma-12B==Qwen-35B), not the gate.
cell_recall is still the HONEST gate (exposes identity-predictors exact-match's no-op inflation masks)
+ the correct fix for the LEARNED-dynamics (TTT) path where the model IS useful (0.55).

